=== layout: subsection

# 15. Drug Scheduling

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. Questions

@body

- Federal Reaction and Drug Scheduling: How is the federal government reacting to state-level legalizations of federally illegal substances like marijuana and psilocybin?
- How does the Controlled Substances Act classify drugs, and what does this mean for state-legalized substances?
- What are the different schedules for drugs?
- What schedules are marijuana and psilocybin classified under?

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. Scheduling Asks a Different Question

@body

- The Controlled Substances Act uses five schedules based on statutory criteria including abuse potential, accepted medical use, and dependence-related considerations.
- Schedule I substances lack currently accepted medical use under the federal scheduling standard. Schedule II substances can have accepted medical use and stringent controls.
- The schedules are legal categories. They are not a simple ranking of how poisonous a substance is or a clinical comparison of every possible use.

@citation

https://www.dea.gov/drug-information/drug-scheduling

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. Prescription, Approval, and Schedule

@body

- FDA approval evaluates a particular product and intended use. Scheduling governs a controlled substance and applicable handling restrictions.
- A prescription requirement is a third issue; most prescription drugs are not controlled substances.
- A lower schedule does not by itself approve every preparation, indication, supplier, or method of sale.
- Ask which substance, product, jurisdiction, and date the statement concerns.

@citation

https://www.dea.gov/drug-information/drug-scheduling

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. Cannabis Needs a Dated Answer

@body

- As of November 12, 2026, the federal order places defined FDA-approved marijuana products and qualifying state-licensed medical marijuana products in Schedule III.
- DOJ states that marijuana outside the order's scope remains under Schedule I controls. Broader rescheduling is a separate proceeding.
- Psilocybin remains federally Schedule I; a state program does not rewrite the federal schedule.
- Do not copy an old table that gives one undifferentiated cannabis classification.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-issuance-federal-registrations-medical-marijuana-dispensaries

=== layout: subsection

# 16. Federal and State Regulation

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. Questions

@body

- Complexity in Regulation: What does the clash between state and federal drug laws reveal about U.S. drug regulation complexity?
- What was the Cole Memorandum of 2013?
- Why was it rescinded in 2018?

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. A State Can Change Its Own Penalties

@body

- State law can remove state penalties or authorize a state-regulated activity without making every version of that activity lawful under federal law.
- Federal and state systems have different authorities, enforcement resources, and policy choices.
- Legalization, decriminalization, and enforcement discretion are different changes.
- Decriminalization often reduces penalties; it does not necessarily create a licensed commercial market.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-issuance-federal-registrations-medical-marijuana-dispensaries

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. What the Cole Memorandum Did

@body

- The 2013 Cole Memorandum described federal marijuana enforcement priorities in states with regulatory systems.
- It was enforcement guidance, not a repeal of the Controlled Substances Act or a guarantee against prosecution.
- The memorandum was rescinded in 2018.
- Later changes require their own sources. A news article about the Cole memo is not a current statement of all federal cannabis policy.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-issuance-federal-registrations-medical-marijuana-dispensaries

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. Read the Fine Print on Legal

@body

- Ask whether the statement refers to possession, cultivation, medical access, commercial sale, research, transport, or federal registration.
- Then identify which government made the change and whether it is a law, a final rule, a proposal, or enforcement guidance.
- The 2026 medical-marijuana order illustrates the difference: state licensure alone does not automatically confer DEA registration.
- The policy debate can be informed by evidence without treating every legal status as a conclusion about safety or benefit.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-issuance-federal-registrations-medical-marijuana-dispensaries

=== layout: subsection

# 17. Marijuana Legalization

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. Questions

@body

- Marijuana Legalization: How has the legalization of marijuana by various states highlighted tensions between state sovereignty and federal authority?
- Which state was the first to legalize marijuana, and in what year did this occur?
- What is the overall direction of marijuana legislation in the United States?
- In states that have legalized marijuana, how they differentiate between medicinal and recreational use?

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. Two Legalization Histories

@body

- California's 1996 initiative is the landmark first modern state medical-marijuana law.
- Colorado and Washington voters approved adult-use legalization in 2012. They answer a different first-state question.
- State programs can differ in qualifying conditions, patient registration, age limits, cultivation, taxation, and retail licensing.
- A map needs a date and a legend before it can answer a legal question.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-issuance-federal-registrations-medical-marijuana-dispensaries

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. A Cannabis Product Is Still a Product

@body

- Different preparations can contain different cannabinoids, concentrations, contaminants, and routes of exposure.
- Evidence about one standardized formulation cannot automatically be transferred to every dispensary product or claimed indication.
- Medical availability through a state program and FDA approval of a specific medicine are different regulatory pathways.
- Separate arguments about criminal penalties from claims about clinical effectiveness.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-issuance-federal-registrations-medical-marijuana-dispensaries

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. What Would You Measure?

@body

- A policy evaluation could examine\
  impaired driving, youth use, poisonings, access to treatment, illicit sales, and enforcement disparities.
- Compare changes over time and plausible comparison populations rather than attributing every change to legalization.
- Benefits, harms, and who bears them are part of the question.
- State trends do not determine the outcome of a pending federal rulemaking.

@citation

https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/justice-department-announces-issuance-federal-registrations-medical-marijuana-dispensaries

=== layout: big-image

@image

![17. The Plant Does Not Specify the Legal Category](assets/set6_sources/topic_17_187.png)

@caption

A photograph cannot tell you the cannabinoid content, manufacturing quality, intended use, or governing license. Those facts change the regulatory question.

=== layout: subsection

# 18. Psilocybin and Other Substances

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. Questions

@body

- Psilocybin and Other Substances: What is psilocybin, and how has its legal status evolved at the state level?
- Which states have decriminalized or legalized psilocybin and other psychedelics ?
- How are new state laws around psilocybin and psychedelics shifting perceptions of their use and benefits?
- What is the future of federal laws around marijuana and psilocybin?

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. Molecule, Treatment, Service

@body

- Psilocybin is converted in the body to psilocin, which acts on serotonin receptors, including 5-HT2A.
- That mechanism helps explain altered perception.\
  It does not establish the benefit or safety of every proposed therapeutic use.
- Clinical research evaluates defined doses, populations, screening, preparation, supervision, and outcomes.
- The molecule, the surrounding support, and the treatment claim must be described separately.

@citation

https://www.oregon.gov/oha/PH/PREVENTIONWELLNESS/Pages/oregon-psilocybin-services.aspx

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. What Oregon and Colorado Changed

@body

- Oregon voters approved a regulated psilocybin-services framework in 2020; the licensed-services system began operating in 2023.
- Colorado voters approved a natural-medicine measure in 2022, with subsequent implementation of a regulated system.
- These frameworks are not unrestricted nationwide retail legalization or FDA approval.
- Use current state program pages for allowed activities and current federal sources for federal status.

@citation

https://www.oregon.gov/oha/PH/PREVENTIONWELLNESS/Pages/oregon-psilocybin-services.aspx

=== layout: one-panel

# 18. Hope Requires a Comparison

@body

- A striking personal report can motivate research but cannot establish the treatment effect by itself.
- Expectation and difficulty maintaining blinding complicate trials of strongly perceptible treatments.
- Screening, adverse events, follow-up, and the durability of benefit matter alongside symptom scores.
- Discuss possible future laws as possibilities. Do not turn a prediction into a present legal fact.

@citation

https://www.oregon.gov/oha/PH/PREVENTIONWELLNESS/Pages/oregon-psilocybin-services.aspx

=== layout: big-image

@image

![18. A Mushroom Photo Is Not an Identity Test](assets/set6_sources/topic_18_190.png)

@caption

Appearance alone does not establish species, composition, dose, or legal status. A defined investigational or regulated product needs a much more specific description.

